{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZ71yAU4VSHQsYwMUhU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dartmouth Provost Santiago Schnell has resigned after a faculty committee found that he used AI to \"meaningfully alter his writing\" and \"was not forthcoming about his AI use.\" The announcement came Wednesday. President Sian Leah Beilock said the departure was \"his decision.\"\n\nThe case began when Semafor ran guest columns in major newspapers through an AI filter. Schnell's August Washington Post column, \"Universities are fighting AI cheating. But there's a deeper problem,\" was found to be entirely written by AI. He said at the time that he used ChatGPT to \"refine a few arguments\" and that the column was based on his own thinking. The student paper, The Dartmouth, then ran his other publications through an AI detector. It found that writing from before ChatGPT's 2022 release was human-written, while his recent writing showed high rates of AI use. When Schnell emailed the paper that he uses AI for \"language refinement, copyediting, improving clarity and organization,\" the paper's detector flagged that email as AI-written too. He then said he had written a draft and used an AI tool to assist with language and clarity.\n\nThe detector results come from a news outlet and a student paper, and the article does not say how reliable those tools are. The faculty committee's finding is a separate judgment, and it centers on disclosure. Beilock's statement says leaders should \"model the transparency and human judgment that we expect from our students and scholars.\" Schnell has referred questions to his attorneys, who had not responded to the AP at publication, so his account of the committee's conclusions is not in the article.\n\nThe context sharpens the issue. The column was about AI cheating, and it appeared as colleges across the country work out how to teach when more assignments can be completed with AI tools. Dartmouth also describes itself as the birthplace of AI, and the conference marking its 70th anniversary, titled \"What Must Remain Human?\", opened the day after the announcement. My reading is that the standard applied here was less about whether AI touched the writing than about how much it changed and whether the author said so. The article does not spell out where the committee drew that line."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2544,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2544},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92b14bfc-b269-494b-a37d-3c81f9d46641","uuid":"45ebcbd8-e5ac-47e0-8706-a1aae71cc9f5","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:32:29.410Z","request_id":"req_011CgdZ71aqpKQvi1rZaBhFc"}
